# Before you start

Learn how the ISA Returns API works and what your organisation needs to consider before using the API.

## API subscription model

You must subscribe each application to the API before it can access the API's endpoints.

## API access control

The ISA Returns API is a Controlled Access API. Endpoints are visible only to applications that are subscribed to it.
To subscribe, you must contact the Software Developer Support Team.

## API versioning

ISA Returns API follows a simplified variation of the semantic versioning to manage changes and maintain compatibility
for API consumers. See our [reference guide](/api-documentation/docs/api/service/disa-returns) for more on versioning.

Users should test and validate against the appropriate API version before requesting access to deploy to the production
environment. To call a specific version of the ISA Returns API, you must:

- confirm that the version is visible on the API’s documentation page
- subscribe your application to that version
- set the correct `Accept` header and `Authorization` header in your API requests

Refer to the [ISA Returns API change log](https://github.com/hmrc/disa-returns/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md) for all important
changes and release information arranged in a reverse chronological order with the latest updates being listed at the
top of the page.

For details on versioning, see the
[Reference guide](https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/api-documentation/docs/reference-guide#versioning).

## API rate limiting

The ISA Returns API has a rate limit of 3 requests per second per ISA manager. You cannot request an increase.

If you exceed this limit, you will receive a `429 Too Many Requests` response. Your application should pause briefly and
retry the request. To avoid further limits:

- follow software design best practices
- avoid batching large volumes unnecessarily
- use randomised backoff timing to reduce spikes

For details on rate limiting, see the
[Reference guide](https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/api-documentation/docs/reference-guide#rate-limiting).

## Authentication

Use your organisation’s Government Gateway user ID to authenticate with HMRC. This grants authority to your application
for the required scopes. HMRC issues an OAuth 2.0 access token, which must be included in requests to user-restricted
    endpoints.

Tokens expire after four hours. You must then request a new one.

For details on user-restricted endpoints, see the
[Authorisation](https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/api-documentation/docs/authorisation/user-restricted-endpoints)
guide.

## Error handling

No system guarantees 100% error-free performance, so services calling our APIs need to be designed with failure in mind.
There are several strategies to mitigate the errors that do occur.

### 5XX errors

If the API returns a `5XX` error, you should retry the request. These errors are usually temporary.

Do not retry `501 Not Implemented` errors. This means the endpoint does not exist or is incorrect. Check the URL.

Use an exponential backoff strategy. For example, try 3 to 5 attempts over 1 to 2 minutes. Consider using a circuit
breaker pattern.

### 4XX errors

`4XX` errors indicate a problem with the request. Check the error message in the response payload and fix the request
before retrying.

A `400` error may mean the payload is invalid. Check against the schema. A `406` error means the `Accept` header is
missing or incorrect.

If you repeatedly receive `4XX` errors, contact the Software Developer Support Team.

Refer to the [API documentation reference guide](/api-documentation/docs/api/service/disa-returns) for general
error-specific information.